# Coding standards

## Type safety

Never typecast. Prefer types the compiler can prove.

Forbidden: `as` (including `as const` and angle-bracket casts), `any`, `unknown`, non-null `!`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, and lint disables used to silence type errors.

Do this: real types at the source, generics, discriminated unions, `typeof` / `instanceof` / `in` / type predicates, schema validation at I/O boundaries, `satisfies`. If the compiler cannot prove a type, fix the types or the API.

## Enums (closed value sets)

If a field can only be one of a known list (gender, status, role), do **not** use a bare `string` or inline `['a', 'b']` in more than one place.

Do **not** use the TypeScript `enum` keyword. This project compiles with `erasableSyntaxOnly` (types must erase to plain JavaScript; `enum` emits extra runtime code). Do not use `as const`.

Gold standard: a string union in `*.types.ts`, the matching array in `*.constants.ts`, schema imports the array. Never put the union and the array in one file. Never paste the list into the model.

```ts
// user.types.ts — types only
export type UserGender = 'female' | 'male' | 'other';

// user.constants.ts — values only
import type { UserGender } from './user.types.ts';
export const userGenders: UserGender[] = ['female', 'male', 'other'];

// user.model.ts — schema only
import { userGenders } from './user.constants.ts';
gender: { type: String, enum: userGenders }
```

Do not type free-form text (name, email, about) as an enum.

## Modular files (hard)

One job per file. If a file has two jobs, split it before adding more code. One rule file only: this file.

Feature layout (flat in the feature folder):

```
src/modules/<feature>/
  <feature>.types.ts       # `type` / `interface` only
  <feature>.constants.ts   # runtime values only
  <feature>.model.ts       # Mongoose schema + model only — "how do I read/write this in Mongo?"
  <feature>.service.ts     # business rules + model calls — "what should the application do?"
  <feature>.controller.ts  # HTTP only — take `req` → call service → `res.status` / `res.json`
  <feature>.routes.ts      # method + URL → controller — "which API calls which function?"
```

Flow: **Route → Controller → Service → Model → Mongo**. Response: **Mongo → Model → Service → Controller → HTTP**.

Do not put DB or business logic in `*.routes.ts`. Do not put Mongo calls in the controller once a service exists. Do not send `res` from the service.

Keep files **flat**. Do not add `types/` or `constants/` subfolders until a group has multiple files. Shared helpers: `src/lib`. Env/DB: `src/config`. Never a root `src/models` or a junk `utils.ts`.

Forbidden:

- Types and `const` values in the same file
- Domain types inside `*.model.ts` (except `InferSchemaType` of that schema — moving it cycles imports)
- Copying a union, interface, or value list into a second file — define once, import
- Redefining `UserGender` (or any closed set) in the model, controller, or route
- Empty files or empty feature folders
- Pre-creating `*.service.ts` (or other layers) with no real export
- Barrel `index.ts` that re-exports the module unless we explicitly add one
- Extra `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` files — all standards live here

`*.types.ts`: only `export type` / `export interface`. No `const`, no functions, no schema.  
`*.constants.ts`: only runtime values. `import type` from `*.types.ts`. Do not declare the union again.  
`*.model.ts`: `import` constants (e.g. `userGenders`). Do not inline `['female', 'male']`.  
`*.routes.ts`: only `router.get/post/...` wired to a controller. No `User.create`, no `req.body` handling.

Create a file only when it has a real export.

## Console logging

Never use raw `console.log`, `console.error`, `console.warn`, or `console.info` in app code. Use the repo logger so the icon shows the outcome:

- ✅ `logger.success` — passed / connected / started
- ❌ `logger.fail` — failed / rejected / crashed
- ⚠️ `logger.warn` — recoverable problem
- ℹ️ `logger.info` — status that is not pass or fail
- 🔎 `logger.debug` — diagnostic detail

Short title plus optional `detail`. Pass the `catch` value as `error`: `logger.fail({ message: 'Failed to sign up', error })`. The logger checks `instanceof Error` and prints the stack. Call sites do not repeat that check. Do not dump unstructured objects or secrets.

## Promises

Every Promise is handled in `try/catch`, including top-level module code.

- `async` functions wrap `await` in `try/catch`.
- Callers `await` inside `try/catch`. No floating Promises. No `void promise`.
- After `catch`, log `fail`/`warn` and recover or rethrow.
- Prefer `async`/`await` + `try/catch` over `.then()` / `.catch()` chains.
- Narrow caught values with `instanceof Error` when the caller uses `.message` / `.stack`. For logging, pass the catch value into `logger.fail({ message, error })` — the logger does that check.

## Named parameters

Named object parameters apply **only to functions we create**.

- Define an `interface` for that object (`LoggerMessageInput`, `ErrorDetailInput`).
- Call our functions as `fn({ name: value })`.
- A function we create with no inputs stays `()`.
- **Do not** wrap, rename, or convert parameters that belong to a library (Express, Mongoose, Node, …) or to TypeScript/JavaScript built-ins. Use those APIs as they are: `app.use((req, res) => { … })`, `mongoose.connect(uri)`.

## Arrow functions

Functions we create are **arrow functions** (`const name = () => { … }`).

Use a `function` declaration only when an arrow cannot do the job (typical case: `this` binding, or a construct that behaves wrongly as an arrow). If you use `function`, add a short comment explaining why the arrow is wrong. Preference is always the arrow.

Library callbacks stay in whatever form the library expects; when we write the callback ourselves, prefer an arrow: `app.listen(port, () => { … })`.

## Comments for uncommon JS / Node

Write comments a frontend engineer new to Node can understand. Use plain English. Spell out jargon the first time (`process`, ESM, TTY, ANSI).

- Explain **why** this exists, and what it does in everyday words.
- Do not use dense compiler flags as the whole comment (`NodeNext`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`) unless you also explain them.
- Do not narrate common `if`, `return`, `try/catch`, named params, or arrows.

Do comment Node-specific things: top-level `await`, `.ts` import paths, terminal color codes, `process` / `process.exit`, TTY checks, `import type`, and any rare `function` used instead of an arrow.

## Git

Commit messages must be clear and descriptive. Write full sentences that explain **why** the change exists, not a vague "wip", "fix", or "update".

Never commit or push files that do not belong on GitHub:

- Secrets: `.env`, passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, Mongo/DB URLs that contain credentials
- Generated or local junk: `node_modules`, `dist`, `coverage`, `.DS_Store`, `*.tsbuildinfo`
- Anything listed in `.gitignore`

Keep secrets in `.env` (gitignored). Never commit real values. If a secret is in source, move it to env **before** committing.
